## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to take ownership under time pressure, address bias ethically, and balance customer impact with product experimentation.

## Key Concepts

- Ownership and accountability in cross-functional triage
- Bias detection, containment, and mitigation in live features
- Trade-off reasoning between hotfix vs rollback vs pausing experiments
- Customer communications and leadership updates without leaking internal data

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: simple bias-mitigating ranking patch
function mitigateBias(ranking, sensitiveFeature) {
  const w = ranking.weights;
  if (sensitiveFeature.confidence > 0.8) {
    w[sensitiveFeature.name] *= 0.75;
  }
  return computeScore(ranking);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the patch across languages and locales?
- What metrics indicate sustained bias reduction after deployment?

## Why This Is Asked
IBM wants to see how you handle change management, influence without authority, and drive technical decisions through data and collaboration rather than just technical expertise.

## Expected Answer
A strong candidate will describe: 1) A specific technical challenge or opportunity, 2) How they gathered data/evidence to support their proposal, 3) How they addressed team concerns through demos or proof-of-concepts, 4) The measurable business impact of the change.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Example: Converting team to TypeScript
interface LegacyAPI {
  data: any;
  status: string;
}

// Improved TypeScript version
interface ModernAPI<T> {
  data: T;
  status: 'success' | 'error' | 'pending';
}

// Proof of concept showing type safety
function processAPI<T>(response: ModernAPI<T>): T | null {
  return response.status === 'success' ? response.data : null;
}
```

